  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 2,
    "lesson_title": "Step Response of a First-Order System",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the step response of a first-order LTI system by convolving a unit-step input with a decaying-exponential impulse response and interpreting the result as a rising exponential toward steady state.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Step Response of a First-Order System",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Step Response of a First-Order System",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The step response of an LTI system is the output produced when the input is the unit step. In this lesson, compute that output directly by convolution: the step input turns the system on, and the impulse response determines the shape of the output.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=u(t), \\quad h(t)=a e^{-a t}u(t), \\quad a>0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the simplest normalized first-order case. The factor $u(t)$ makes the impulse response causal, and the factor $e^{-a t}$ makes it decay after the impulse. The step response is the convolution $y(t)=(u*h)(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=(u*h)(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}u(\\tau)a e^{-a(t-\\tau)}u(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The unit steps set the active part of the integral. The factor $u(\\tau)$ requires $\\tau \\ge 0$, and the factor $u(t-\\tau)$ requires $\\tau \\le t$. These two conditions overlap only when $t \\ge 0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t\\ge 0:\\quad y(t)=\\int_0^t a e^{-a(t-\\tau)}\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $t \\ge 0$, the integral accumulates more of the decaying impulse response as $t$ grows. So the output starts at 0 and rises toward its steady-state value instead of staying flat or decaying from 1.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=(1-e^{-a t})u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use this pattern for the rest of the lesson: set the causal overlap bounds, evaluate the exponential integral, attach the final unit-step gate, and interpret the result as a rising exponential step response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Step Response of a First-Order System",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Setting Up the Unit-Step Input Convolution",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Set up the convolution integral for the step response of a first-order LTI system with input $x(t)=u(t)$ and impulse response $h(t)=2e^{-2t}u(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that this is a step response of an LTI system, so the output is the convolution of the unit-step input with the impulse response. Start from the convolution formula and keep $t$ fixed while $\\tau$ is the integration variable.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute the two functions into the integrand. The input becomes $x(\\tau)=u(\\tau)$, and the shifted impulse response keeps the shifted argument $t-\\tau$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}u(\\tau)\\,2e^{-2(t-\\tau)}u(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The unit-step factors determine the bounds. The factor $u(\\tau)$ is nonzero only when $\\tau \\ge 0$, and $u(t-\\tau)$ is nonzero only when $\\tau \\le t$. There is no overlap when $t<0$; when $t\\ge 0$, the overlap interval is $0\\le \\tau \\le t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\begin{cases}0, & t<0,\\\\ \\displaystyle \\int_0^t 2e^{-2(t-\\tau)}\\,d\\tau, & t\\ge 0.\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the setup only. The integrand and bounds are now correct, and the exponential integral is left unevaluated for the next step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Step Response of a First-Order System",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Evaluating the First-Order Step Response Integral",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate the step response for $x(t)=u(t)$ and $h(t)=2e^{-2t}u(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Setup: the previous step already found the causal overlap interval. For $t<0$, there is no overlap, so the output is 0. For $t\\ge 0$, evaluate the integral over $0\\le \\tau \\le t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t\\ge 0:\\quad y(t)=\\int_0^t 2e^{-2(t-\\tau)}\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Integration: rewrite the exponent so the dependence on the integration variable is clear. Since $t$ is fixed during the integration, $e^{-2t}$ can be treated as a constant factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "2e^{-2(t-\\tau)}=2e^{-2t}e^{2\\tau}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute that form into the integral and integrate with respect to $\\tau$ from 0 to $t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned} y(t)&=\\int_0^t 2e^{-2t}e^{2\\tau}\\,d\\tau \\\\ &=2e^{-2t}\\left[\\frac{1}{2}e^{2\\tau}\\right]_0^t \\\\ &=e^{-2t}(e^{2t}-1) \\\\ &=1-e^{-2t}. \\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Causal form: the calculation gives the positive-time branch. Attach $u(t)$ to include the zero-output region for $t<0$ in the final step response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=(1-e^{-2t})u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The key move is to keep the causal bounds from the setup, pull out the fixed factor $e^{-2t}$, and simplify the evaluated integral into the rising-exponential form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Step Response of a First-Order System",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Writing the Causal Step Response",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A first-order step-response calculation gives $y(t)=2(1-e^{-3t})$ for $t\\ge 0$. Write the full causal response in unit-step form and piecewise form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the calculation only gives the positive-time branch. Do not redo the convolution or change the expression; the task is to add the missing causal information.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=2(1-e^{-3t}),\\quad t\\ge 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For compact causal notation, multiply the entire positive-time response by $u(t)$. The unit-step factor gates the whole expression, so the response is zero before the step turns on.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=2(1-e^{-3t})u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The equivalent piecewise form states the same causal behavior explicitly: zero before $t=0$, and the given positive-time formula after the step turns on.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\begin{cases}0, & t<0,\\\\ 2(1-e^{-3t}), & t\\ge 0.\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Both forms preserve the same positive-time step response. The unit-step form is compact, while the piecewise form makes the zero-output region visible.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Step Response of a First-Order System",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Interpreting the Rising Exponential Toward Steady State",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For the step response $y(t)=2(1-e^{-3t})u(t)$, identify the initial value, steady-state value, time constant, and qualitative behavior.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the first-order step-response form $K(1-e^{-a t})u(t)$. For interpretation after the step turns on, use the positive-time branch and read the multiplier, exponential rate, and limiting behavior from that formula.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t\\ge 0:\\quad y(t)=2(1-e^{-3t})",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "First find the initial value just after the step turns on. Substitute $t=0^+$ into the positive-time expression.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(0^+)=2(1-e^0)=2(1-1)=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Next find the steady-state value by taking the limit as $t$ grows. The decaying exponential term goes to 0.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\lim_{t\\to\\infty} y(t)=2\\left(1-\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}e^{-3t}\\right)=2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The time constant is the reciprocal of the exponential rate. Here the rate is 3, so the time constant is $1/3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\text{time constant}=\\frac{1}{3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the response has initial value 0, steady-state value 2, and time constant $1/3$. Qualitatively, it rises exponentially toward 2 rather than decaying to 0.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
